  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 AM MDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nHave you ever had someone that overstayed their welcome, be it at a \nparty or staying at your house? Well, the weather is doing that \nagain. Our friend the monsoon (which those of you who read my \ndiscussions know I love so much, note sarcasm) has returned. And \nwill be around for a few more days.\n\nWe do have some shower and isolated thunderstorms around as I write \nthis, but nothing strong and the storms are moving. Monsoonal \nmoisture will continue to push northward today and increase the \ncoverage of showers and storms across the area. Coverage still looks \nscattered at best though (at most a 1 out of 3 chance). There is \ndecent model consensus in keeping a vast majority if the showers and \nstorms along and west of the Continental Divide and also near Casper. \nWe can't rule a shower or storm out anywhere, but the chance is only \naround 1 in 10 or less. As for potential flooding, probably not \nthrough Wednesday night. The core of the deepest moisture should \nremain south of Wyoming today. There is also some steering flow, \nabout 15 to 20 knots worth, which should keep the showers and storms \nof the moving and not of the stationary variety. As for temperatures,\nthis looks to be the warmest day of the week with flow turning \nto south and still some sunshine. Some of the warmer spots will \nlikely see highs around or over 80 degrees.\n\nCoverage should increase on Thursday. An upper level low will slowly \napproach from the Pacific Northwest. At the same time, a strong \nridge over the southeast will begin to retrograde to the west and \ndirect deeper moisture into the area, as much as 175 percent of \nnormal. At the same time, a weak shortwave will be pivoting around \nthe upper level low giving a bit more lift. However, there is a \nlimiting factor. It is that with the moisture in the atmosphere, \nmuch of the day may end up being fairly cloudy. This may limit \ninstability somewhat, especially since it is now mid September and \nnot July, limiting daytime instability. Nevertheless, there will be \nan increased chance of showers, especially across western Wyoming. \nWith the increased cloud cover, temperatures will also be cooler, \ngenerally by about 5 to 10 degrees compared to Wednesday.\n\nThe upper level low will begin to make a closer approach by Friday. \nOf all the days, this looks like the most active day across the area \nwith almost all areas having at least a 1 in 3 chance of convection \nduring Friday and Friday evening. I have one more concern about this \nday as well. Most guidance is showing a 20 percent drop in \nprecipitable water. However, this could lead to a fairly sunny \nmorning that could bring more instability and a greater chance of \nconvection, especially East of the Divide. If there could be local \nflooding, this could the most likely day. The one mitigating factor \ncontinued to be steering flow though, as much as 40 knots at times. \nSo the storms will be moving. And with more sun, temperatures will \nbe warmer, but likely not as warm as today.\n\nThe low will passing close to the area on Saturday,turning flow more \nto the northwest. This should result in a noticable cooler day \nacross the area. There will still be enough wrap around moisture for \nsome showers and isolated thunderstorms to continue. However, most \nguidance shows precipitable water values much lower on this day, so \nthe threat of heavy rain looks less. Uncertainty then increases from \nSunday into early next week. as a lot depends on the movement of the \nupper low. Some guidance moves it far enough east for drier weather \nto move in for Sunday, other guidance keeps showers around through \nSunday night. Confidence is not high in specifics as the models have \nhad different solutions in regards to speed and track of the upper \nlevel low for the past few days. However, we do have high confidence \nof unsettled weather through at least Saturday. Somewhat drier and \nwarmer weather may then return for early next week.",
